<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Late Middle Ages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> is the period of European and wider Eurasian history from about 1300 to about 1500. It saw expanding long-distance trade, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> and major political changes. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> note that Mongol patronage of commerce and communication laid foundations for the exchange networks of this period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages.jpg" alt="Late Middle Ages" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F. A. Brockhaus for Alfred A. Knopf · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europe_in_the_14th_Century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term refers to the last of three periods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, after the early and High Middle Ages. Leonardo Bruni was the first historian to use this threefold division, in his History of the Florentine People of 1442, and Flavio Biondo used a similar framework in his Decades of History. The division became standard after the German historian Christoph Cellarius published his Universal History in 1683. Some historians, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, prefer not to speak of a late Middle Ages at all, and see the High Middle Ages passing straight into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and the modern era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Around 1300 to 1350 the Medieval Warm Period gave way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-ice-age">Little Ice Age</a>. The colder climate caused agricultural crises, the first known as the Great Famine of 1315–1317, and later came the plagues, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, for which estimates of the death rate range from one third to as much as sixty per cent. By about 1420 recurring plagues and famines may have reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s population to no more than a third of what it had been a century earlier, and it took 150 years to regain the level of 1300. Peasant uprisings such as the Jacquerie and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a> of 1378 to 1417 added to the strain.</p><p>Military practice changed. Battles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> in 1302, Bannockburn in 1314 and Morgarten in 1315 showed that feudal cavalry had lost its advantage to well-equipped infantry, and the English longbow served them against the French. Cannons used as siege weapons altered the design of fortifications. In trade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> saw a commercial revolution in the late 13th and early 14th centuries, with new forms of partnership, insurance, bills of exchange and double-entry bookkeeping. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> peaked in the 14th century and declined in the fifteenth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The century also brought progress in the arts and sciences. Humanism challenged scholasticism, the early 14th-century painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> prefigured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> art, and in 15th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> patrons such as the Medici supported painters and sculptors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-ockham">William of Ockham</a> insisted that reason and faith be kept apart. The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1453 sent Byzantine scholars to the West, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> spread learning. Ottoman expansion cut off trade with the East and drove Europeans to seek new routes, leading to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s voyage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> in 1492 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1498. Many scholars therefore see the period as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, though the division is somewhat artificial, since ancient learning was never entirely absent from European society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Late Middle Ages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mongol patronage of commerce and communication prepared the wider networks of exchange of the <b>Late Middle Ages</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the <b>Late Middle Ages</b> the Great Pyramid had a reputation as a haunted structure.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>L'homme armé (&quot;the armed man&quot;) is a secular song of the <b>Late Middle Ages</b>, from the Burgundian School.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)</a></li></ul></section>
